{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–12 EUR), especially after the market turns down.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model extrapolates prior uptrend and reacts too slowly to regime shifts; it keeps prices elevated while the true series sells off.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following spikes/high levels in the recent history, the forecast path remains near the peak or rebounds too strongly, while outcomes mean-revert downward.",
    "Underestimates drawdown magnitude and speed: declines in the true series are sharper and deeper than forecast; apply stronger downside adjustment when recent momentum turns negative.",
    "Horizon effect: error grows with lead time—near-term forecasts are closer, but mid/late-horizon paths drift upward relative to outcomes; increase downward correction with forecast horizon.",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast is too smooth and centered high; outcomes show larger downside volatility—widen downside risk and shift central path lower in bearish regimes."
  ]
}
